# Web client

- The shared game certificate warning compares the installed certificate SANs to panel hosts, including one-label DNS wildcards and IP addresses.

- The only UI: React 19, TypeScript (strict), Vite, Tailwind v4, Radix dialogs, TanStack Query, TanStack Router (code-based route tree in `src/router.tsx`, routes code-split with `lazyRouteComponent`), TanStack Virtual for log windowing.
- Wiring: `src/main.tsx` builds `AppServices` (`src/app/services.ts`: API client, endpoints, `SessionStore`, `SessionRefresher`, QueryClient) and renders `AppRoot`, which reacts to session events. Tests build the same services over a fake `Transport`; never add module-level singletons.
- API layer (`src/api`): `client.ts` owns `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest` for panel API calls; every failure is an `ApiError` (`errors.ts`) parsed from the error envelope or mapped to `network_error`/`invalid_response`/`aborted`. Components show `describeError(err)` and the request id, never response text. All wire types come from `types.gen.ts` (generated; run `scripts/gen-types.sh`). Add routes to `endpoints.ts`, query keys to `query-keys.ts`, hooks to `queries.ts`.
- Session: token in memory, mirrored to sessionStorage (default) or localStorage ("Keep me signed in"), key `golem_deploy_session`; tabs sync over the `golem_deploy_session` BroadcastChannel. A 401 `unauthenticated` for the current token ends the session once (`SessionStore.handleUnauthenticated`). `SessionRefresher` refreshes 30 minutes before the JWT `exp` and on tab return after long inactivity. The displayed role comes from `GET /account`, never from the JWT.
- First admin: `/setup` (`SetupPage` in `src/pages/auth.tsx`, outside the signed-in routes; a signed-in visitor is redirected to `/`) takes the token from `src/lib/setup-token.ts`: the first statement of `main.tsx` moves a `/setup#t=` token into that module-level holder and replaces the history entry without the fragment, before the router or session exist (a fragment-only change on `/setup` reloads the page; `renderApp` does the same capture on the memory history). The holder is cleared when the token is used up or refused or `setup_required` is false; the token never goes back into a URL or storage. The page waits for `GET /auth/options` (no form while loading), says at once that an old link is used up when `setup_required` is false (with the sign-in link), and posts `POST /setup` with the registration form's rules; success stores the session like login and goes to `/`. No token or `setup_link_invalid` shows the plain explanation and `SETUP_LINK_COMMAND`; the login page shows a notice with it while `setup_required`. Keep the command in step with the installer's service user and data directory.
- Credentials only as `Authorization: Bearer`; never cookies. Streams (job logs, server logs) use `client.stream` + `readLines` (`src/api/stream.ts`) through `useLogStream`; the contract has no resume offset, so streams are not auto-reconnected.
- React Query: no retry on 4xx, polling only for state that changes on its own or through other users and only while visible: job, project, server and project-build lists use `listPollInterval` (2 s while something is in progress, 10 s otherwise), plus job and build details, server detail, stats, the runtime view (only while `state` is `running`, no retries) and status. A finished job invalidates builds too (a start records the schema fingerprint). Per-row actions own their mutation so pending state stays per row. Every job-returning action shows the job inline (`InlineJob`).
- UI primitives live in `src/components/ui`; reuse them instead of new class strings. Render server strings as text; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`; links from server data go through `safeHttpUrl`.
- The SPA's CSP allows `connect-src 'self'` plus HTTPS and WSS to the effective game host. Keep the build free of inline scripts, eval and remote assets. Only the browser connection test (`src/lib/connection-test.ts`) opens direct game connections, with no API credentials or game messages. It reads `/connectivity` and `/runtime` on demand and closes all probes on success, failure, timeout and navigation. A browser without WebTransport can still run HTTPS and the WebSocket fallback. After a successful WebSocket probe, report UDP reachability and an unserved WebTransport path as possible causes of WebTransport failure.
- Settings uses separate game, panel and ACME certificate queries and refreshes them while visible to pick up renewals. Keep the reported mode while a restricted issue job runs and show its status, detail and log through `InlineJob`. Terminal certificate jobs refresh the certificate and server queries. A changed panel certificate prompts a reload. Warn about the mounted key when a shared game certificate and the panel use the game host.
- Contract mirrors: client validators (`validateOrigin` in `src/components/origins-editor.tsx`, `validateAddonTarget` in `src/components/static-addons.tsx`, `validateGameHost` in `src/pages/admin-settings.tsx`, `checkServerImport` for export format 2 in `src/lib/server-import.ts`, `src/lib/validators.ts`: repo URLs with the daemon's wording and `sameRepoOrigin` (an origin change requires new auth), env keys (only `GOLEM_` is reserved) with case-insensitive uniqueness, env values (single line, 32 KiB per variable counted in bytes, 128 variables / 64 KiB per set), static names, ports; `authTypesFor` offers no "none" for SSH URLs) and `jobPermissions` (`src/components/jobs.tsx`: restricted jobs, cancel by creator or admin) copy daemon rules for early feedback only; still handle the daemon's 4xx where the action happens. Change them together with the Go rule they mirror.
- Errors: `describeError` explains codes the daemon cannot (`bad_host`, `invalid_credentials`, ...) and otherwise shows the envelope message; `withWait` merges `Retry-After` into it as one sentence (a trailing "try again later" is replaced, not repeated). A 429 on opening a log stream is shown inside the `LogViewer` (`notice`) with a retry. Field errors go where the field is: `auth.*`/`auth` in project dialogs, `env.<KEY>`/`env_set.<KEY>` to the env editor row (`envErrorsFrom`), `allowed_origins[i]` to the origin row (`originErrorsFrom`), `static_addons[i].name|target` to the add-on row, `build_id` on the build select, `game_host`, `certificate_pem`/`private_key_pem` on their fields, `servers[i].*` named in the import dialog. `job_in_progress` and every other new failure (build details, start failures, 409 reasons of build/project/image/static deletes) are shown as the daemon worded them, never replaced by generic text. Job and build details are plain text and wrap in full.
- Builds: the project page's Builds section starts builds (`Pull first` is forced while there is no checkout, otherwise off) and lists them newest first; a build is a `project.build` job of the project, shown with `InlineJob`; `/builds/$id` shows details and the build job's log. Builds carry only `created_by` (an id): the username comes from the project's job list. Admins delete builds; static add-ons and `allow_custom_dockerfile` are admin edits on the project page.
- Servers are created from `GET /builds?status=success` (project select, newest build preselected; `/servers?create=<project id>` opens the dialog for a project); with none, explain the project, build, server order instead of showing a form. `GettingStarted` shows that order while no server exists. A server's build changes only through the deploy dialog (other successful builds of its project; any build for a server without one); for a running server it offers PATCH then `POST /restart` and says players are disconnected. `Server.certificate` and `GameCertificate.certificate` may be null in every mode: show both cases. The private key of a certificate upload lives only in the form and is cleared on success. Actions follow `state` (`status-badges.tsx`: `portLocked`, `serverActionsBlocked`): the port is locked while the container runs/restarts/is paused or the state is `unknown` (Docker unreachable for a server with a container), `unknown` disables start/stop/restart/delete and log reads, `missing` (no container) offers start but no restart or logs. `DELETE /servers/{id}` waits for its `server.delete` job: success is a toast, a failure is an error toast plus the job shown inline. The delete dialog (`DeleteServerDialog`) says that a last backup (kind `pre_delete`) is stored first and kept; admins can tick "Skip the last backup" under "More options" (`skip_safety_backup=1`). A 409 `conflict` (the last backup could not be taken or would be incomplete; nothing deleted) keeps the dialog open with the daemon's message and the admin's way out, and the job is still shown inline. Server logs arrive sanitised: the viewer dims escaped sequences (`\x1b[31m`, `\u202e`) and the job log's `container | ` prefix (`logSegments`) and never interprets them.
- Static files: the page shows `used_bytes`/`quota_bytes` (0: unlimited)/`file_count`/`max_files` and warns when queued files cannot fit (`fitWarning`). One file per request, so each row's status is its request's all-or-nothing outcome (5xx and network errors: "outcome unknown"); `createLimiter` in `client.ts` caps upload requests of the whole tab at `MAX_CONCURRENT_UPLOADS` (2, the daemon's per-account cap) and the page waits out a 429 from uploads elsewhere. `replace=1` only for files the user confirmed; a 409 offers Replace on that row; a 507 marks the file not stored.
- Saved data and backups: the server page's "Saved data and backups" section (`src/components/server-data.tsx`) and the `/backups` page share the table and dialogs in `src/components/backups.tsx`. Backup lists refresh every 10 s (scheduled backups appear on their own) and when a server job ends. A build's fingerprint comes from the successful-builds list; `schemaMatch` reports a difference only when both sides are known. Restore sends `force` only after the checkbox that appears when the schemas are known to differ or the daemon answers 409 `schema_mismatch`; "skip the safety backup" stays under "More options"; reset requires the server name typed. Restore consent (force, skip and the daemon's refusal) is kept per target server, build and both fingerprints and cleared when any of them changes; a chosen server that leaves the list clears the selection instead of falling back to another server. With known different schemas the steps say the server stays stopped. Kinds are labelled in words (`backupKindLabel`, including `pre_delete`); `omitted_entries` above 0 is shown as "N entries left out" in the tables (explained in the title) and as a warning in the restore dialog. The Taken, Kind and Size values do not wrap; Note and Project give way. Downloads above 512 MiB (`LARGE_DOWNLOAD_BYTES`) first warn that the browser may fail and show the `curl` command with an API token. Restore, reset and backup deletion are admin actions; download uses the bearer header (`saveBlob`). The deploy dialog warns, without blocking, when the chosen build cannot load the saved world. `validateSnapshotInterval`, `validateBackupInterval`, `validateBackupKeep` and `validateBackupNote` (after `trimBackupNote`, like Go's `strings.TrimSpace`; refuses `BACKUP_NOTE_FORBIDDEN`: C0, DEL, C1, U+2028/U+2029 and bidirectional formatting characters, like `servers.ValidateNote`) in `src/lib/validators.ts` and the import fields in `src/lib/server-import.ts` mirror the daemon's ranges; `backup_schedule` is always sent whole. Field errors on `snapshot_interval_seconds`, `backup_schedule.*` and `note` go to their fields. Durations are written in words without zero parts (`formatDurationWords`). `src/test/backups.test.tsx` pins these flows.
- Inspector: `src/components/inspector.tsx` holds the admin Settings section (`#inspector`: state in words, `available` false offers no controls, turn on/off, "More options" for `pull_image` and `rotate_secrets`, turn-off and replace-secrets confirmations, the `inspector.apply` job via `InlineJob` and the subject's job list), the server page's section (the server's `inspector` switch, sent as `inspector` on create and PATCH, and its state from `serverInspectorState` in `src/lib/inspector.ts`: switch off (for a running server with `inspector_active` and a running Inspector: the Inspector no longer shows it, the game keeps trying to connect until its next restart, with the restart action), installation Inspector off or not running, game servers cannot reach it (`agents_reachable` false: an administrator changes how the daemon is started, no restart advice), server state unknown, not running, restart needed when `inspector_active` is false, unknown when `targets` is null, not connected with changes waiting for a restart when `restart_required` (never blamed on the Inspector settings: the API cannot tell what changed), connected per `targets`, not connected yet) and `OpenInspectorButton` (also on the Servers header while the Inspector runs). Settings words `other_targets` as entries that belong to no server with the switch on (a running server whose switch was turned off counts there). A PATCH refused with 409 `conflict` (not available) keeps the daemon's message while the section refreshes to "Not available on this host". `useInspector` polls 2 s while a mounted query knows an active `inspector.apply` job, 10 s otherwise; a finished one invalidates the Inspector and the servers. Opening it: the tab is opened in the click handler (`about:blank`, `opener` cleared; a tab whose opener cannot be cleared is closed and never gets the link), navigated to `inspectorEntryUrl` (`public_url` or this page's scheme and host with `port`, plus the absolute `path`, whose `#t=` fragment holds the ticket and must be kept) when `POST /inspector/sessions` answers before the link's deadline (the earlier of request time plus 60 s and `expires_at`), closed when it fails (`toast.error`) or the deadline passed (the dialog then shows the expired state). Without a usable tab (blocked, closed by the user, opener kept) the dialog offers a button, never a link element, that opens the link with `window.open(url, "_blank", "noopener,noreferrer")` until the deadline, checked again on every click; an expired link is dropped and "Get a new link" asks for another. The link is kept only in component state (no mutation cache), never put in the page. User text says "the Inspector", "the game server" and "link". `src/test/inspector.test.tsx` pins these flows. The Inspector's own pages are not part of this client: `src/test/inspector-scripts.test.ts` runs the daemon's `internal/inspector/boot.js` and `enter.js` in jsdom (loaded with `?raw`; `vitest.config.ts` allows that directory).
- Mock layer: `src/mock/transport.ts` stands in for the Docker-, git- and file-backed routes (projects, builds, servers and their runtime and connectivity metadata, images, static, volumes, certificate settings, the Inspector and jobs; the game host is the page's host name) under `VITE_API_MOCK=1 npm run dev` (`GOLEM_DEPLOY_DEV_API` overrides the dev proxy target), following the final contract (access levels via the real `GET /account`, restricted jobs, follow cap, tail bounds, replace semantics, first-error import validation of format 2, SSH auth and origin rules, env field names, port lock, `job_in_progress` for port and build changes, same-project builds, build lifecycle and delete rules, runtime 409/503, certificate modes and field errors, add-on rules, `unknown` state, static usage/quota/upload cap, escaped log output, backup note rule, restore protection of a backup only while the restore runs, `missing` state after a reset or a forced restore of a mismatched backup, `pre_delete` backups and the admin-only `skip_safety_backup=1` (exactly one of `1`, `true`, `0`, `false`; empty or repeated is 400), deletion only after a delete job that ended in success, safety backups with omitted entries that are stored and then fail the job, putting back a server that was running with the daemon's job log lines, the Inspector's access levels (read by any identity, PATCH admin, sessions only for login sessions), links of the daemon's form (`/_golem-deploy/enter#t=<43 base64url characters>`, the server not in the link), its restricted `inspector.apply` job with `job_in_progress` and the 400 for `pull_image`/`rotate_secrets` while off, the daemon's defaults (image `ghcr.io/demiurgos-hub/golem-inspector:0.4.0-rc.1`, port 8444), state `off` as soon as the setting is off (also while the turn-off job runs), `image_id` only once the hub exists and `started_at` whenever it runs, no download while the host has the image, targets for running servers started with the Inspector and its current secrets whose switch is still on, matched by name to servers with the switch on (the rest in `other_targets`; a game that left stays listed as not connected for 30 s), sessions checked in the daemon's order (server: `null` is absent, 0 or less is unknown; then the setting on and running, so 409 as soon as a turn-off is stored; then at most 8 unused links per account, 429 with `Retry-After`, forgotten on a turn-off and on new secrets), `inspector_active`, and `restart_required` like the config hash: the Inspector variables a running container was started with (switch, installation on, secrets generation) against what it would get now, plus other pending changes (`restartPending`)). Its seed includes failure cases (a failing build, a crashing start, a silent admin endpoint, a server without a build, a server whose data has entries a backup leaves out, a server started with the Inspector whose game never connects). `src/mock/transport.test.ts` pins those rules. Keep it in step with contract changes or delete the domain from it; it must never behave differently from the daemon. It is imported only behind `import.meta.env.DEV`; `vite.config.ts` refuses to build with the flag and fails if the mock reaches the bundle.
- Verification: `npm run lint` (zero warnings), `npm test` (Vitest + Testing Library, jsdom; `src/test/contract.test.tsx`, `src/test/contract-updates.test.tsx` and `src/test/milestone3.test.tsx` pin contract behaviour; wire fixtures with every field are in `src/test/fixtures.ts`), `npm run build` (type-check + bundle for Go embedding). For UI changes that touch the daemon, also run the built binary against a temp data dir and exercise the flow in a browser (a project, a build, a server started from it, its runtime view and game URL).
